Overview
This directory contains the complete Wazuh detection configuration for the PGH-SOC project, including 47 custom rules across five detection domains and 2 custom decoders for log parsing. The rule set covers PRSociety authentication monitoring, Mailcow phishing detection, suspicious command execution, Active Directory attacks, and Linux audit-based AD tool detection.

Custom Decoders
Decoder Name	Parent	Log Source	Purpose
prsociety	— (root)	PRSociety web application	Parses JSON logs beginning with {"app_service":"prsociety"
audit-bloodhound	auditd	Linux auditd (EXECVE)	Extracts the executed command (a1=) from auditd EXECVE events
Decoder 1: PRSociety JSON Decoder
xml
<decoder name="prsociety">
  <program_name>json</program_name>
  <prematch>^{"app_service":"prsociety"</prematch>
</decoder>
Matches logs starting with {"app_service":"prsociety".

Uses Wazuh's built-in JSON decoder to extract all key-value pairs automatically.

Fields such as username, src_ip, auth-status, and password_length are inherited directly from the JSON keys.

No <order> tag is required because the JSON decoder handles extraction automatically.

Decoder 2: Auditd BloodHound Decoder
xml
<decoder name="audit-bloodhound">
  <parent>auditd</parent>
  <prematch>type=EXECVE</prematch>
  <regex offset="after_prematch">a1="(\S+)"</regex>
  <order>audit.arg1</order>
</decoder>
Inherits from the built-in auditd decoder.

Matches EXECVE events (command execution).

Extracts the first argument (a1="...") into the field audit.arg1.

Used by rules 112001 and 112002 to detect BloodHound and Kerberoasting activity.

Detection Domains
Domain	Rule IDs	Purpose
PRSociety Authentication	110001–110014	Login success/failure, brute force, rate limiting, distributed attacks
Mailcow Phishing	120001–120021	Suspicious subjects, sender spoofing, malicious links, sensitive info requests
Suspicious Commands	110050–110051	CDB list-based command monitoring
AD Enumeration	110400–110402	Group enumeration (Event ID 4799), Administrators group targeting
AD Kerberos & Auth	110100–110106	TGT/TGS requests, failed auth, account lockout, admin privileges
AD Account Management	110200–110204	Account creation, deletion, enable/disable
Kerberos Brute Force	110300–110301	Multiple pre-auth failures from same IP
AD Attack Tools (Linux)	112001–112002, 110500–110508	BloodHound, Kerberoasting, DCSync, Mimikatz, Rubeus, Impacket, CrackMapExec
